  [38;5;153msearch          1.12.0    2/3     2026-09-29 10:10   2 failing[39m
  [38;5;153m...[39m

  [38;5;153mFailing checks:[39m
    [38;5;153mbilling        db-connection     timeout after 5s[39m
    [38;5;153msearch         disk-space        91% used[39m
    [38;5;153msearch         index-freshness   last update 47 min ago[39m
  - Gains: you can spot problems at a glance, the table stays narrow, and you see the [38;5;153mdetail[39m text explaining why each
    check fails.
  - Costs: the output gets a second section.

  [1mOption B: HEALTH column only, naming the failing checks[0m
  [38;5;153mbilling         4.2.3     2/3     2026-09-28 17:40   failing: db-connection[39m
  [38;5;153msearch          1.12.0    2/3     2026-09-29 10:10   failing: disk-space, index-freshness[39m
  - Gains: a single table.
  - Costs: rows get wide and uneven, and the [38;5;153mdetail[39m text doesn't fit anywhere.

  [1mOption C: HEALTH column with a status word only ([38;5;153mok[39m / [38;5;153mfailing[39m)[0m
  - Gains: the smallest change.
  - Costs: you can see that a service is failing but not which check failed or why.

  All three are cheap to change later. I recommend [1mA[0m: the column answers "which services are failing?" and the list
  answers "why?" without making the table wider. If no services are failing, I'd leave the "Failing checks" section out
  entirely.
